  "summary": "The code contained a critical OS command injection vulnerability, improper HTTP method handling, and poor coding standards. The refactored code implements strict input validation, uses subprocess for safe execution, and adheres to PEP 8 naming conventions.",
  "fixed_code": "import subprocess\nfrom flask import Flask, request, abort\n\napp = Flask(__name__)\n\nALLOWED_COMMANDS = {'status', 'restart'}\n\n@app.route(\"/command\", methods=['POST'])\ndef execute_command() -> str:\n    '''\n    Receive a command from a post request, \n    validate it against an allowlist, and execute it safely.\n    '''\n    data = request.get_json()\n    action = data.get('action') if data else None\n    \n    if not action or action not in ALLOWED_COMMANDS:\n        abort(400, description=\"Invalid or missing action\")\n\n    try:\n        subprocess.run(['/usr/bin/safe_binary', action], check=True, capture_output=True)\n        return \"Command executed successfully\"\n    except subprocess.CalledProcessError:\n        abort(500, description=\"Command execution failed\")"
